The 12d Programming Language (12dPL) uses C-style syntax but varies from C.
Programs written in 12dPL are referred to as 12d macros, or simply macros.
> 1-based indexing
> all programs start in void main()
> global variables are declared in {}
> variables and functions cannot have the same name
> there are no pointers
> there are no classes (unless I specify there is)
> Does not share types with C
> Does not share functions with C
> Switches support Integer64/Integer/Text/Real types
> Text supports == operator
> 'c' is an Integer, "c" is Text
> Variable declared in for loop header exists beyond the for loop
> No short-circuit evaluation, every condition is checked

12d Environment
> There are none-to-many views
> Text is not supported in Perspective view
> Project has tins and models, Models can have elements and tins, Elements are various things but the simple one is super string, which has vertexes. Models and tins can be displayed on views. Tins can exist without models, I won't recommend it.

Panels
> Widgets are created, then appended, then the panel is shown using Show_widget

