My formatting expectations
> You should be able to gleam this from the scaffold provided but I like stuff aligned, tend towards meaningful variable names almost always rather than unreadable abbreviations, functions are written like so:
<return_type> <function_name>(<parameters,...>)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Explanation of function use
// any other important details
// ----------------------------------------------------------------
{
  // code...
}

As there are no constants, for Text/Real/Integer/Integer64/etc is it preferred to use object-like macros. For example with return codes I like to use SUCCESS (0) and FAILURE (-1) as my generic ones, or ATTR_REFERENCE_STRING ("reference_string") to keep the code protected from a mismatch in text. These sorts of things should reside in the appropriate header file.

My custom structure
the intent is to obey Model-View-Controller architecture, and have everything be event based, for loose coupling purposes. Each file should include the files it will uses (helps with Visual Studio Code syntax highlighting). "build.4dm" is where all source files are included, and it should work no matter the order if each file is including the required headers.

Preprocessor - Function-like macros
This is experimental concept, but consider
Function-like macro:
STR_TOKEN(I01) #I01
STR_TOKEN(thing)
"thing"

My concept:
#define PARAM_I01
#include "STR_TOKEN(PARAM_I01)"

the file content of "STR_TOKEN(PARAM_I01)"
#ifndef PARAM_I01
#error PARAM_I01 not defined
#endif

#ifndef STR
#define STR(I01) #I01
#endif

STR(PARAM_I01)

#undef PARAM_I01

This example does not demonstrate the advantages too well because it is so simple but the advantage of this method is for one the lack of \ everywhere (neater, cleaner), and that is that the error code will appear in the file "STR_TOKEN(PARAM_I01)" with the include line also mentioned (more debugging info, nothing lost), rather than an error at column 50000 at some place that is not the definition of the macro, at only the cost of some boilerplate and unconventional usage
this method also distinguishes these things from regular functions, which is safer

Debugging
#define DVAR1(I01) Print("DEBUG | "); Print(#I01); Print(I01); Print();
#define D2(I01, I02) Print("DEBUG | ");  Print(I01); Print(I02); Print();
This is the simplest debugging, but you can imagine D1-D8, DVAR1-DVAR8, and you can also imagine that if DEBUG is not defined then these are defined with no replacement. These live in their own header.

Attributes
You'll likely be very well off using attributes to store data about objects.
Basically everything in 12d can have attributes. Attributes can have attributes. The project can have attributes. Attributes can store text and numbers.
You could create a form of custom datatypes by creating attribute groups that have a child attribute called "datatype" and putting whatever you want in there, and so long as your program understands what you're talking about then it's effectively a data structure.
